      "application_cycle": "annual NOFA",
      "next_deadline": "2026-27 cycle: NOFA posted December 8, 2025, applications due January 23, 2026 at 4:00 pm, virtual workshop December 17, 2025, Board public hearing May 12, 2026. The 2027-28 NOFA is expected in December 2026 with a late January 2027 deadline; confirm with Neighborhood Preservation at (209) 468-3175 or neighborhood@sjgov.org.",
      "prevailing_wage_trigger": "true",
      "layering_notes": "San Joaquin County has no county housing trust fund, no county housing bond and no inclusionary ordinance; the County's own capital for multifamily is limited to HUD pass-through funds administered by the Human Services Agency Neighborhood Preservation Division (sjchsa.org), while the regional gap lender is the SJCOG-sponsored San Joaquin Regional Housing Fund and the main voucher and development partner is the Housing Authority of the County of San Joaquin (HACSJ). The Urban County covers unincorporated San Joaquin County plus Escalon, Lathrop, Manteca, Ripon and Tracy (qualification package for FY 2027-28 through 2029-30 authorized August 4, 2026); Stockton and Lodi are separate HUD entitlements with their own NOFAs (Pass 3). Roughly half of CDBG and HOME passes through to the five cities, so County HOME available for rental development is typically under $1 million a year including program income; the 2026-27 draft Action Plan says requests far exceeded funds. HOME is used as a small soft loan behind 4% and 9% tax credit deals. Lodi Commons (Housing Authority, 55 units, TDC $28,463,882) stacks $3,031,559 of County HOME from three fiscal years, $2,000,000 County HOME-ARP, $1,000,000 County HHAP, $5,400,000 HACSJ funds, $665,000 City of Lodi CDBG, $2,000,000 Health Net, $3,000,000 San Joaquin Regional Housing Fund, $500,000 HUD CPF and about $10.7 million LIHTC equity, with 32 PBVs and 22 ACC units (FONSI dated November 4, 2026). Davis-Bacon applies to HOME-assisted projects of 12 or more units and to CDBG-assisted projects of 8 or more units; state prevailing wage applies to public subsidy. The Neighborhood Preservation pages moved from sjgov.org to sjchsa.org in 2026; the 2025-2029 Consolidated Plan and 2026-2027 Annual Action Plan are posted there.",
      "source_url": "https://www.sjchsa.org/Portals/0/resources/neighborhood-preservation/home/2026-2027-Public-Notice-Notice-of-Funding-Availability-(NOFA)-English.pdf",
